1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,601
Garlic that takes forever to peel, bread
gone

2
00:00:03,611 --> 00:00:06,640
hard, brown sugar like a rock.

3
00:00:07,790 --> 00:00:10,552
A few small tricks fix all of that.

4
00:00:10,913 --> 00:00:14,200
Eight of them, tested and safe, that save

5
00:00:14,210 --> 00:00:15,986
time and cut waste.

6
00:00:16,346 --> 00:00:19,947
Stay for step four: it brings stale bread

7
00:00:19,957 --> 00:00:21,233
back to life.

8
00:00:21,867 --> 00:00:22,818
WikiTube.

9
00:00:23,051 --> 00:00:24,896
Whatever it is, here's how.

10
00:00:25,167 --> 00:00:28,663
Professional cooks rely on small habits
and shortcuts

11
00:00:28,673 --> 00:00:30,738
that never appear in recipes.

12
00:00:31,030 --> 00:00:34,024
No gadgets, no secret ingredients.

13
00:00:34,315 --> 00:00:37,939
They simply make everyday jobs faster,
safer or

14
00:00:37,949 --> 00:00:41,608
tastier, from peeling garlic in seconds to
rescuing

15
00:00:41,618 --> 00:00:42,929
hard brown sugar.

16
00:00:43,476 --> 00:00:46,493
Everything you need is already in your
kitchen.

17
00:00:47,033 --> 00:00:49,415
You'll need a chef's knife, a damp kitchen

18
00:00:49,425 --> 00:00:52,213
towel, a glass jar with a lid, a

19
00:00:52,223 --> 00:00:55,136
microwave, and a bowl of cold water.

20
00:00:55,590 --> 00:00:58,561
Take care with knives and hot items.

21
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:00,086
Trick one.

22
00:01:00,447 --> 00:01:03,444
A sliding cutting board is dangerous,
because it

23
00:01:03,454 --> 00:01:04,823
makes knives slip.

24
00:01:05,486 --> 00:01:07,624
Wet a kitchen towel or a few sheets

25
00:01:07,634 --> 00:01:10,247
of paper towel, wring it out so it's

26
00:01:10,257 --> 00:01:13,336
damp but not dripping, and fold it flat

27
00:01:13,346 --> 00:01:14,517
under your board.

28
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:18,119
The damp fabric grips the counter and
holds

29
00:01:18,129 --> 00:01:20,055
the board firmly in place.

30
00:01:20,416 --> 00:01:24,479
It works on any smooth surface, and takes
five seconds.

31
00:01:25,629 --> 00:01:29,436
Whenever you chop, use a firm, stable
board.

32
00:01:30,401 --> 00:01:32,028
This is the first trick to use every

33
00:01:32,038 --> 00:01:35,566
day, because it makes every other kitchen
task safer.

34
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:37,179
Trick two.

35
00:01:37,981 --> 00:01:40,641
Place a garlic clove on a board, lay

36
00:01:40,651 --> 00:01:42,720
the flat side of a large knife over

37
00:01:42,730 --> 00:01:45,994
it, and press down firmly with the heel

38
00:01:46,004 --> 00:01:46,990
of your hand.

39
00:01:47,351 --> 00:01:50,450
The skin cracks, and slides right off.

40
00:01:50,810 --> 00:01:53,399
For a whole head, break it into cloves,

41
00:01:53,527 --> 00:01:55,549
put them in a lidded jar or two

42
00:01:55,559 --> 00:01:59,645
bowls, and shake hard for ten to fifteen
seconds.

43
00:02:00,098 --> 00:02:03,627
The cloves rub against each other and lose
their skins.

44
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:06,564
Keep your fingers away from the blade
edge.

45
00:02:06,925 --> 00:02:09,701
A silicone garlic peeler tube also works
if

46
00:02:09,711 --> 00:02:11,405
you cook with garlic often.

47
00:02:12,370 --> 00:02:14,508
Roll the clove back and forth, and the

48
00:02:14,518 --> 00:02:15,805
skin comes off.

49
00:02:16,770 --> 00:02:19,811
Next, a quick test for the eggs in your
fridge.

50
00:02:20,367 --> 00:02:21,446
Trick three.

51
00:02:21,807 --> 00:02:24,003
Fill a bowl or a tall glass with

52
00:02:24,013 --> 00:02:27,646
cold water, and gently place the egg in
it.

53
00:02:28,448 --> 00:02:30,609
A very fresh egg sinks to the bottom

54
00:02:30,619 --> 00:02:32,406
and lies on its side.

55
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:36,504
An older egg sits at an angle or stands
upright.

56
00:02:36,865 --> 00:02:39,189
If it floats, it's likely too old to

57
00:02:39,199 --> 00:02:41,276
use, so throw it away.

58
00:02:42,078 --> 00:02:44,553
As eggs age, they lose moisture through
the

59
00:02:44,563 --> 00:02:47,952
shell, and the air pocket inside grows.

60
00:02:48,313 --> 00:02:51,888
Always cook eggs thoroughly, and keep them
in the fridge.

61
00:02:52,341 --> 00:02:55,057
Use the freshest eggs for poaching and
frying,

62
00:02:55,186 --> 00:02:59,434
and slightly older ones for boiling, which
peel more easily.

63
00:02:59,967 --> 00:03:00,930
Trick four.

64
00:03:01,221 --> 00:03:03,751
Don't throw away bread that has gone hard.

65
00:03:04,042 --> 00:03:06,332
Run a loaf quickly under the tap, so

66
00:03:06,342 --> 00:03:08,781
the crust is slightly damp, and put it

67
00:03:08,791 --> 00:03:11,498
in a hot oven at about 160 degrees

68
00:03:11,508 --> 00:03:13,922
Celsius, or 320

69
00:03:15,072 --> 00:03:17,126
Fahrenheit, for ten minutes.

70
00:03:17,673 --> 00:03:21,120
The moisture turns to steam, softens the
inside

71
00:03:21,214 --> 00:03:22,873
and crisps the crust.

72
00:03:23,420 --> 00:03:25,871
For a sliced loaf, wrap slices in a

73
00:03:25,881 --> 00:03:29,108
damp paper towel and microwave for ten
seconds.

74
00:03:29,469 --> 00:03:34,042
Eat it right away, since revived bread
goes stale quickly.

75
00:03:35,007 --> 00:03:37,505
Bread revived this way is best used the

76
00:03:37,515 --> 00:03:41,786
same day, for toast, sandwiches or
croutons.

77
00:03:42,751 --> 00:03:45,469
The next trick rescues something else
people throw

78
00:03:45,479 --> 00:03:48,137
away: hard brown sugar.

79
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:49,721
Trick five.

80
00:03:50,082 --> 00:03:52,452
Brown sugar turns into a rock when its

81
00:03:52,462 --> 00:03:53,854
moisture dries out.

82
00:03:54,145 --> 00:03:56,074
To fix it, place the lump in a

83
00:03:56,084 --> 00:03:58,985
bowl, cover it with a damp paper towel,

84
00:03:59,219 --> 00:04:02,968
and microwave in ten-second bursts until
it softens.

85
00:04:03,329 --> 00:04:05,118
Or seal it in a container with a

86
00:04:05,128 --> 00:04:09,307
slice of fresh bread, or an apple wedge,
overnight.

87
00:04:09,969 --> 00:04:12,325
The sugar absorbs moisture from them.

88
00:04:12,686 --> 00:04:14,705
Remove the bread the next day.

89
00:04:15,670 --> 00:04:20,011
To prevent hardening, store brown sugar in
an airtight container.

90
00:04:20,465 --> 00:04:22,707
Brown sugar works best when stored in an

91
00:04:22,717 --> 00:04:26,004
airtight jar, with a piece of terracotta
or

92
00:04:26,014 --> 00:04:27,627
a marshmallow inside.

93
00:04:28,167 --> 00:04:29,211
Trick six.

94
00:04:30,176 --> 00:04:32,729
Fresh herbs wilt fast in the fridge.

95
00:04:33,090 --> 00:04:37,190
Treat soft herbs, such as parsley,
cilantro and

96
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:39,173
dill, like flowers.

97
00:04:39,835 --> 00:04:42,287
Trim the stems, put them in a jar

98
00:04:42,297 --> 00:04:44,899
with an inch of water, loosely cover the

99
00:04:44,909 --> 00:04:48,031
leaves with a bag, and keep them in the
fridge.

100
00:04:48,392 --> 00:04:50,701
Change the water every couple of days.

101
00:04:51,666 --> 00:04:54,719
Basil is different, because it hates the
cold.

102
00:04:55,683 --> 00:04:57,461
Keep basil in a jar of water on

103
00:04:57,471 --> 00:05:00,210
the counter, away from direct sun.

104
00:05:01,361 --> 00:05:03,960
Herbs stay fresh for a week or more.

105
00:05:04,252 --> 00:05:07,040
Hardy herbs like rosemary and thyme can be

106
00:05:07,050 --> 00:05:09,733
wrapped in a damp paper towel, and kept

107
00:05:09,743 --> 00:05:11,286
in a bag in the fridge.

108
00:05:12,251 --> 00:05:14,284
Now for a trick that gets more out

109
00:05:14,294 --> 00:05:15,501
of every lemon.

110
00:05:16,033 --> 00:05:17,077
Trick seven.

111
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:19,959
Before you cut a lemon or lime, roll

112
00:05:19,969 --> 00:05:23,288
it firmly on the counter, pressing down
with your palm.

113
00:05:23,951 --> 00:05:26,818
This breaks the little juice sacs inside.

114
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:30,199
For even more juice, warm the whole fruit

115
00:05:30,209 --> 00:05:33,029
in the microwave for ten to fifteen
seconds,

116
00:05:33,053 --> 00:05:34,283
then roll it.

117
00:05:35,433 --> 00:05:37,592
Cut it in half and squeeze.

118
00:05:38,046 --> 00:05:39,847
You can get a lot more juice from

119
00:05:39,857 --> 00:05:40,982
the same fruit.

120
00:05:42,132 --> 00:05:44,221
Remove the seeds before cooking.

121
00:05:44,675 --> 00:05:48,076
Use a fork to twist and squeeze every last
drop.

122
00:05:48,530 --> 00:05:50,853
A hand juicer or fork helps you squeeze

123
00:05:50,863 --> 00:05:54,046
the very last drops, and the zest freezes

124
00:05:54,056 --> 00:05:55,286
well for later.

125
00:05:55,833 --> 00:05:56,749
Trick eight.

126
00:05:57,412 --> 00:06:00,815
Lower eggs gently into boiling water, and
cook

127
00:06:00,825 --> 00:06:03,439
for nine to twelve minutes, depending on
how

128
00:06:03,449 --> 00:06:04,923
firm you like the yolk.

129
00:06:05,284 --> 00:06:07,096
Move them straight into a bowl of ice

130
00:06:07,106 --> 00:06:08,684
water for five minutes.

131
00:06:08,975 --> 00:06:11,206
The shock of cold makes the egg shrink

132
00:06:11,216 --> 00:06:14,930
slightly away from the shell, so it peels
easily.

133
00:06:15,732 --> 00:06:17,928
Tap the egg on the counter, roll it

134
00:06:17,938 --> 00:06:20,135
gently under your palm to crack the shell

135
00:06:20,145 --> 00:06:23,429
all over, and peel under a little running
water.

136
00:06:24,092 --> 00:06:26,610
The shell comes off in large pieces.

137
00:06:27,157 --> 00:06:29,701
Older eggs are actually easier to peel
than

138
00:06:29,711 --> 00:06:32,093
very fresh ones, so this is a good

139
00:06:32,103 --> 00:06:34,018
use for the ones in your fridge.

140
00:06:34,567 --> 00:06:36,412
Every trick has a limit.

141
00:06:37,563 --> 00:06:39,826
Very fresh eggs are the hardest to peel,

142
00:06:39,850 --> 00:06:42,147
so use eggs that are a week or two old.

143
00:06:43,298 --> 00:06:46,538
Revived bread goes stale again fast, so
eat

144
00:06:46,548 --> 00:06:47,755
it the same day.

145
00:06:48,209 --> 00:06:51,043
Brown sugar hardens again, so keep a slice

146
00:06:51,053 --> 00:06:52,829
of bread in the container too.

147
00:06:53,375 --> 00:06:56,593
Cold citrus gives less juice, so use fruit

148
00:06:56,603 --> 00:06:58,076
at room temperature.

149
00:06:58,633 --> 00:07:01,035
Freezing saves a lot of food.

150
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,683
Freeze lemon juice in ice cube trays, and

151
00:07:04,693 --> 00:07:08,280
keep ginger whole in the freezer, then
grate it frozen.

152
00:07:09,082 --> 00:07:11,765
Chop spare herbs, press them into a tray

153
00:07:11,775 --> 00:07:14,189
with olive oil, and freeze them.

154
00:07:15,339 --> 00:07:18,232
Slice bread before freezing, so you can
toast

155
00:07:18,242 --> 00:07:20,203
just one slice at a time.

156
00:07:20,866 --> 00:07:23,433
Label everything with the date, and use it

157
00:07:23,443 --> 00:07:24,928
within a few months.

158
00:07:25,467 --> 00:07:28,195
A safe kitchen starts with a sharp knife,

159
00:07:28,370 --> 00:07:30,296
because a dull blade slips.

160
00:07:30,657 --> 00:07:33,617
Keep a separate cutting board just for raw
meat.

161
00:07:34,164 --> 00:07:36,917
Wash your hands, boards and knives with
hot

162
00:07:36,927 --> 00:07:39,538
soapy water after touching raw meat.

163
00:07:40,503 --> 00:07:43,729
Put leftovers in the fridge within two
hours of cooking.

164
00:07:44,090 --> 00:07:46,901
Use the oldest food first, and check the

165
00:07:46,911 --> 00:07:49,163
dates on packages before you cook.

166
00:07:49,700 --> 00:07:52,346
Before you try these, a few cautions.

167
00:07:53,148 --> 00:07:56,284
First, always cut away from your hands,
and

168
00:07:56,294 --> 00:07:58,604
keep your fingers clear of the blade.

169
00:07:59,569 --> 00:08:01,866
Fast does not mean careless.

170
00:08:03,017 --> 00:08:06,058
Second, be careful with microwaved food.

171
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:09,404
Steam and hot items can burn you, so

172
00:08:09,414 --> 00:08:12,153
use short bursts, and test the heat.

173
00:08:13,303 --> 00:08:16,750
And third, don't take shortcuts with food
safety.

174
00:08:17,042 --> 00:08:20,895
Throw away eggs that float, and keep your
eggs refrigerated.

175
00:08:21,256 --> 00:08:24,123
A great trick is one that is also safe.

176
00:08:24,577 --> 00:08:28,027
If something feels dangerous, skip the
trick, since

177
00:08:28,037 --> 00:08:30,300
no shortcut is worth an injury.

178
00:08:30,833 --> 00:08:32,864
Let's recap the eight tricks.

179
00:08:33,318 --> 00:08:35,395
Stop your cutting board from sliding.

180
00:08:36,197 --> 00:08:38,019
Peel garlic in seconds.

181
00:08:39,169 --> 00:08:40,910
Test eggs for freshness.

182
00:08:42,060 --> 00:08:43,766
Revive stale bread.

183
00:08:44,219 --> 00:08:46,169
Soften hard brown sugar.

184
00:08:46,971 --> 00:08:48,723
Keep herbs fresh longer.

185
00:08:48,956 --> 00:08:50,836
Get more juice from citrus.

186
00:08:51,197 --> 00:08:54,261
And peel boiled eggs easily with ice
water.

187
00:08:55,226 --> 00:08:57,700
Try one tonight, and you'll wonder how you

188
00:08:57,710 --> 00:08:59,091
ever cooked without it.

189
00:09:00,241 --> 00:09:04,515
Bonus sections cover failed tricks,
freezer ideas and

190
00:09:04,525 --> 00:09:05,906
safe kitchen habits.

191
00:09:06,467 --> 00:09:09,368
Those are eight kitchen tricks you'll wish
you knew earlier.

192
00:09:09,729 --> 00:09:13,177
If one saved you time, subscribe to
WikiTube,

193
00:09:13,352 --> 00:09:16,752
because we publish a new how-to every
single day.

194
00:09:17,299 --> 00:09:19,135
Tell us in the comments which trick was

195
00:09:19,145 --> 00:09:21,408
new to you, and share your own.

196
00:09:22,558 --> 00:09:23,800
See you tomorrow.

197
00:09:23,900 --> 00:09:25,199
That's WikiTube.

198
